---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import CatStage from "../components/CatStage.astro";
import AttentionTrace from "../components/AttentionTrace.astro";
import DownloadCta from "../components/DownloadCta.astro";
import { languageAlternates, softwareApplicationSchema, websiteSchema } from "../lib/site";

const title = "Blink Reminder App with On-Device Detection | Lumi";
const description = "Lumi detects complete blinks on your device and reminds you after a long gap. It also supports 20-20-20 and stand-up breaks on Mac, Windows, and Linux.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/"
  alternates={languageAlternates("/", "/zh-cn/")}
  schemas={[websiteSchema, softwareApplicationSchema]}
>
  <SiteHeader currentPath="/" alternatePath="/zh-cn/" />

  <main id="main-content">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="shell hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">ON-DEVICE BLINK DETECTION · MACOS, WINDOWS & LINUX</p>
          <h1>Lumi notices <span class="soft-line">long gaps between blinks.</span></h1>
          <p>Lumi detects complete blinks on your computer. After a long gap, a small prompt asks you to blink slowly twice. Camera frames are processed in memory and discarded.</p>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a class="button" href="/download/">Download Lumi</a>
            <a class="button button-secondary" href="/blink-reminder/">How blink detection works</a>
          </div>
          <p class="hero-note">Distance-break and stand-up reminders are included and can be switched off separately.</p>
        </div>
        <CatStage />
      </div>
      <div class="shell"><AttentionTrace /></div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">HOW THE BLINK REMINDER WORKS</p>
            <h2 id="why-heading">From a complete blink to a two-blink reminder.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>Blink sensing runs only while monitoring is active and the face signal is usable. You can pause it at any time, and locking or sleeping the computer releases the camera.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof-grid">
          <article class="proof-card">
            <span>01 / DETECT</span>
            <h3>Confirm a complete blink</h3>
            <p>Lumi looks for an open–close–open sequence across both eyes. One uncertain frame does not count as a blink.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="proof-card">
            <span>02 / WAIT</span>
            <h3>Measure the gap</h3>
            <p>Every detected complete blink restarts the no-blink interval. The reminder appears only after that gap becomes prolonged.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="proof-card">
            <span>03 / REMIND</span>
            <h3>Blink slowly twice</h3>
            <p>Two detected blinks complete the prompt. Lumi then starts a cooldown so the reminder does not immediately return.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-tint" id="features" aria-labelledby="features-heading">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">OTHER REMINDERS AND RECORDS</p>
            <h2 id="features-heading">Choose what Lumi keeps track of.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>Blink, distance, and sedentary reminders have separate switches. A master monitoring switch and schedule control when camera sensing can run.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-rail">
          <article class="feature-row">
            <span class="feature-time">AFTER A LONG GAP</span>
            <h3>Respond to a long blink gap</h3>
            <p>A small prompt asks for two slow blinks. Complete them to dismiss it, or turn the blink reminder off without changing the other features.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-row">
            <span class="feature-time">EVERY 20 MINUTES</span>
            <h3>Look beyond the screen</h3>
            <p>A calm 20-second horizon appears after 20 minutes of observed screen time. Skip it when needed, or use the free browser timer before installing.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-row">
            <span class="feature-time">YOUR INTERVAL</span>
            <h3>Stand up on your schedule</h3>
            <p>Choose a sedentary interval from 1 to 600 minutes. Lumi distinguishes a completed stand-up movement from a brief tracking loss.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-row">
            <span class="feature-time">LAST 30 DAYS</span>
            <h3>Review a local activity timeline</h3>
            <p>Explore blink, screen, posture, and yawn events by day. The timeline lives in IndexedDB on your computer and automatically drops records older than 30 days.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="rule-heading">
      <div class="shell horizon-panel">
        <img src="/assets/horizon-break.webp" width="1400" height="420" loading="lazy" alt="A soft illustration of distant hills and a lake at sunrise" />
        <div class="horizon-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">20 · 20 · 20</p>
          <h2 id="rule-heading">Look at a distant object for 20 seconds.</h2>
          <p>The American Optometric Association recommends looking at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds after every 20 minutes of screen use to help ease digital eye strain.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/tools/20-20-20-timer/">Start the free browser timer<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-dark" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
      <div class="shell privacy-layout">
        <div class="content-block">
          <p class="eyebrow">CAMERA DATA STAYS ON YOUR DEVICE</p>
          <h2 id="privacy-heading">Frames are processed in memory and discarded.</h2>
          <p>MediaPipe runs on your computer. Lumi saves small behavior events in a local timeline for 30 days. It does not save photos, video, face coordinates, or screen contents.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="privacy-ledger">
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Processed</span><strong>Camera frames, in memory, on this device</strong></div>
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Stored</span><strong>Six event types and paired screen/posture intervals</strong></div>
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Never stored</span><strong>Photos, video, raw landmarks, or screen contents</strong></div>
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Retention</span><strong>Thirty days, removed automatically</strong></div>
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Account</span><strong>None</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="difference-heading">
      <div class="shell privacy-layout">
        <div class="quote-panel">
          <p class="eyebrow">VISIBLE FEEDBACK</p>
          <h2>The cat blinks when Lumi detects one.</h2>
          <p>The small desktop companion mirrors detected blinks and shows reminder state without turning your activity into a score.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="content-block">
          <p class="eyebrow">YOUR CONTROLS</p>
          <h2 id="difference-heading">Every reminder has its own switch.</h2>
          <p>Lumi does not assign streaks or health scores. You can hide the desktop companion, pause monitoring, or disable any reminder independently.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:20px"><a class="text-link" href="/blink-reminder/">Explore the blink reminder<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-tint" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <div><p class="eyebrow">BEFORE YOU INSTALL</p><h2 id="faq-heading">Questions about sensing and privacy.</h2></div>
          <p>Lumi supports screen habits. It is not a medical device and cannot tell you why your eyes feel uncomfortable.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details>
            <summary>Does Lumi diagnose or treat dry eye?</summary>
            <p>No. It can remind you to blink and take breaks, but it cannot diagnose dry eye disease, measure tear quality, or replace an eye examination. Read the <a href="/dry-eyes-from-screen/">dry eyes and screen use guide</a>; persistent pain, redness, light sensitivity, discharge, or sudden vision change needs professional care.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Does it record me or capture my screen?</summary>
            <p>No. Camera frames are processed in memory and discarded. The app does not capture screen content. Its local timeline stores small behavior events such as a detected blink or the start and end of an observed screen interval.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What happens when the camera cannot see me?</summary>
            <p>Blink and posture sensing pause, and the app explains that sensing is unavailable. It does not replace the missing signal with a hidden timer.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can every reminder be turned off?</summary>
            <p>Yes. Blink, distance, and sedentary reminders have independent switches, plus a master monitoring switch and configurable monitoring windows.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is the app signed?</summary>
            <p>Not yet. Current builds are free preview releases and may trigger macOS Gatekeeper or Windows SmartScreen warnings. The download page explains the limitation before you install.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <DownloadCta />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
